  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half its value as a surface with contour lines; it is 3 times steeper left-right, so overshoot shows there first. Without noise, steps diverge left-right once the learning rate is above 2/3.",
    "Noise is Gaussian, centred on the true slope, with spread 1.5·sqrt((64−b)/(63·b)) per direction: largest at batch 1, exactly zero at 64.",
    "Orange arrow: the actual move, −learning rate × noisy slope. Grey arrow: −learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn 2× their true length.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or is marked diverged when the loss passes 6 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. The trail holds the start plus up to 40 landings; the ghost keeps one earlier run.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed and a counter that keeps going across new runs, so each new run gets fresh noise and Reset replays the same sequence. Off-bowl points are drawn at the bowl's edge."
  ]
}